prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages employee expense reports and reimbursement disbursement.

The people involved are employees, line managers, finance reviewers, and controllers.

One person may hold both the employee and line manager role at the same time.

The resources are expense reports and reimbursements.

Every expense report records its submitter, approver, total amount, meal total, lodging total, category, amount tier, whether a receipt is attached, its status, and when it was submitted.

Every reimbursement records its expense report, who disbursed it, and the amount.

Each requester carries an approval tier.

Each request carries whether it is on the corporate network, the current time, and whether it is an emergency approval.

During business hours, a controller may disburse a reimbursement only when the expense report has approved status.

Outside business hours, the approved-report requirement does not bind the controller.

A controller must not disburse a reimbursement for an unapproved expense report during business hours.

A request by a controller to disburse a reimbursement that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity Person {
    approvalTier: String,
};

entity Employee {
    person: Person,
};

entity LineManager {
    person: Person,
};

entity FinanceReviewer {
    person: Person,
};

entity Controller {
    person: Person,
};

entity ExpenseReport {
    submitter: Employee,
    approver: LineManager,
    totalAmount: decimal,
    mealTotal: decimal,
    lodgingTotal: decimal,
    category: String,
    amountTier: String,
    receiptAttached: Bool,
    status: String,
    submittedAt: datetime,
};

entity Reimbursement {
    expenseReport: ExpenseReport,
    disbursedBy: Controller,
    amount: decimal,
};

action disburseReimbursement appliesTo {
    principal: [Controller],
    resource: [Reimbursement],
    context: {
        onCorporateNetwork: Bool,
        currentTime: datetime,
        emergencyApproval: Bool,
        businessHours: Bool,
    },
};
"
}
